owing two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2-i\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2+i\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534759 true] [17592186134350 1145 "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{1+i})+2\\cdot \\overset{―}{3}+(2-i)(\\overset{―}{2+\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot (1-i)+2\\cdot 3+(2-i)(2-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2+6+(3-4\\text{i}) \\\\  & =11-4i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534759 true] [17592186134471 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534759 true] [17592186134471 1100 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ over $C$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $⟨a, b⟩ = ⟨b, a⟩^(―)$ for any $a, b ∈ C^{n}$\n2. $⟨a, αb⟩ = α⟨a, b⟩$ for any $a, b ∈ C^{n}$ and any $α ∈ C$\n3. $⟨a, b⟩ ∈ R$ for any $a, b ∈ C^{n}$" 13194139534759 false] [17592186134471 1100 "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨a,b⟩\\in R$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$" 13194139534759 true] [17592186134471 1145 "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is false. To factor out a constant multiple from the second argument, we must change it to its complex conjugate.\n\n- Statement III is false. The inner product over $C$ is a complex number in general.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534759 true] [17592186308163 1017 #edb/ref 17592186308164 13194139534759 true] [17592186308163 1089 "q-141039" 13194139534759 true] [17592186308163 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534759 true] [17592186308163 1100 "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{4},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1+2i \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2+i \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534759 true] [17592186308163 1113 #uuid "7180dc99-97c3-575a-b7f9-b8879ffd2435" 13194139534759 true] [17592186308163 1145 "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}}+{x}_{4}\\overset{―}{{y}_{4}} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\overset{―}{(2+i)}+2\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-3})+(1+2i)\\cdot \\overset{―}{1}+1\\cdot \\overset{―}{2} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (2-i)+2\\cdot (-3)+(1+2i)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(-2+i)+(-6)+(1+2\\text{i})+2 \\\\  & =-5+3i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534759 true] [17592186308164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308165 13194139534759 true] [17592186308164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308166 13194139534759 true] [17592186308164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308167 13194139534759 true] [17592186308164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308168 13194139534759 true] [17592186308164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186308169 13194139534759 true] [17592186308164 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 1319413